   "turn": 6,
   "response_text": "Now I have a good understanding. Let me analyze the situation:\n\n1. **Label noise fraction: 0.42** - This is extremely high. 42% of the training labels have been randomly flipped. This is fundamentally problematic for training because:\n   - The model receives contradictory signals about the same patterns\n   - The training loss cannot decrease much because no matter what the model learns, ~42% of samples will be mislabeled\n   - This causes the validation accuracy to be highly volatile and the model to struggle\n\n2. **Training loss**: Only decreased from 0.6888 to 0.6834 (0.7% improvement) over 20 epochs, which is extremely minimal.\n\n3. **Validation accuracy**: Highly volatile (ranging from 0.797 to 0.838), which is a symptom of the noisy/corrupted training labels.\n\nThe issue is that with 42% label noise, the model cannot achieve good generalization because it's being trained on systematically corrupted data. This is not a typical training configuration issue - it's an intentional but problematic data configuration.\n\nLet me verify if this is the intended behavior by checking if there's documentation or if this is indeed a fault:\n\nLooking at the configuration, the `data.label_noise_fraction` parameter is set to 0.42, which is extremely high and clearly problematic. A typical label noise level would be 0-0.1. At 0.42, you're flipping labels on nearly half your training data, which makes it impossible for the model to learn effectively.\n\nHowever, I need to determine if this is:\n1. A misconfiguration (an operator error)\n2. An intentional adversarial test case\n\nGiven the context that this is a diagnostics task and the workload is \"tabular_adult\" (a standard benchmark), and that:\n- The training curve shows extremely poor learning (loss barely decreasing)\n- Validation accuracy is very noisy and not converging\n- The label_noise_fraction of 0.42 is unrealistically high for any practical use case\n- Reference runs typically use noise fractions of 0.0 or very small values\n\nThis appears to be a misconfiguration. The label_noise_fraction should be a much smaller value, typically 0.0 (no noise) or at most 0.05-0.1 for a controlled robustness test.",
